“Our decision hasn’t been new.
We had already made an explanation as a juncture in July, when there was a question about Pagėgiai and Utena.
At that time, we said, according to the articles ofia, this is a matter of the competence of the Governing Board.
And if the Governing Board decides that any junction can behave as it pleases - to go with the committee and the Governing Board approves, then the department can go, but if the Board decides that there will always be party lists of what the board has taken, then there is a decision that all divisions would form party lists.
And if you don't agree with the board, then there's a possibility to suspend your membership and go with the committee," he added.
Tamošiūnas said the party's board will now decide on further actions.
On October 17, the Board of the Liberal Sąjūdis of the party revoked the decision of the Vilnius City Division to participate in the municipal elections not with the list of the party, but with the newly formed electoral committee.
According to the board of the party, the decision of the liberals of the capital to participate in the elections in the form of a public electoral committee violated the party’s articles of association and the decisions of the board.
Until October 31, the Vilnius branch had to cancel the aspirations to participate in municipal elections with a committee list.
But the department didn't.
Aušrinė Armonaitė, the former head of the division, has already left the Liberal Movement.
Remigijus Šimašius, the Mayor of Vilnius, the former Chairman of the Liberal Movement, who promises to pursue another term of office of the head of the capital, has already presented the team and ideas of the public electoral committee "Behind Vilniai, to which we are proud of.
He left the party as well.
Municipal councils and mayoral elections will take place in March next year.
Residents of a village in northern India were riving a tiger with a trawler that previously mauled a man, officials said on Monday.
Formal care official Mahvir Kaujlagi said the tigreys had mauled a man who worked in the fields late on Sunday, the villagers encirculated the beast as a trawler while trying to escape.
Residents say the tiger also attacked and mascared for one young man about 10 days ago.
The village is located in the Dudhva tiger sanctuary of Lakhympur Khereri, about 250 kilometers southeast of Uttar Pradesh's capital Laknau.
It is forbidden to use tigers in protected areas.
Ramesh Pandey, director of the apprentice Ramesh Pandey, said police would be notified under the Wildlife Conservation Act.
In April, villagers were battling another tiger in Nighassan district of Uttar Pradesh state in Nighassan, who suffered serious injuries.
Tiger is an Indian national animal; under the Wildlife Conservation Act tiger is considered an endangered species.
According to last year's government's data, at least one person was killed every day during collisions with wild tigers or elephants over the previous three years.
People's tiger conflicts have been gradually increasing since the 1970s, when India embarked on a tiger-protective program that houses reserves were set up in national parks, and the sharpening of big cats began to be considered a crime.
Although tiger calculation methods have evolved, censuses would show that the then population of about 1,800 tigers increased to 2,226 individuals in 2014.
Proposed mandatory microchip marking of cats and dogs
Kęstutis Mažeika, Chairman of the Seimas Committee on Environmental Protection, together with other members of the Seimas, has registered amendments to the Law on Animal Welfare and Conservation, in which it is proposed that cats and dogs must be branded with subcutaneous microchips.
"We propose that cats and dogs must be labeled with subcutaneous microchips, and their registration and marking would be organized by local municipalities, the Ministry of Agriculture and the State Food and Veterinary Service.
In 2019, all dogs will be labelled free of charge with public funds, and in 2020, cats will not have any concerns and costs will be covered by the specially provided state funding," said Kęstutis Mažeika, Chairman of the Seimas Committee on Environmental Protection.
Legislative amendments aim to solve the systemic need for the labelling of domestic animals.
It's hoped it will help to avoid problems caused by irresponsible animal breeders, unidentified multipliers or sellers.
Preliminarily it is estimated that, according to the expected average price of the microchip and service, which will amount to about 5 euros, will be reimbursed to all animal owners during the transitional period.
The overall labelling price for all pet animals can reach up to EUR 6 million.
It is planned that the need for these funds will be distributed within three years and will amount to EUR 2 million annually.
It is planned that funds will be allocated to the labelling programmes through the special programme of support for the protection of the environment of municipalities, whose income this year will significantly increase due to increased taxes on natural resources and redistribution of funds.
Municipalities will be able to use these funds not only for the marking of cats and dogs, but also for castration and other programs.
According to K. Mažeika, Mandatory labelling practices are widespread in almost all countries of the European Union, which makes it possible to protect the animals themselves (because those who are lost can easily be returned to the owners) and saves the time of the animal breeders themselves, when it takes a large amount of time and funds to the search for an evacuated pet.
In addition, EU legislation envisages travel only with labelled pets.
At the same time, marking obliges responsibly take care of the animal throughout its life.
The previous proposals for mandatory denigration attracted resistance, because the relevant programs were proposed to be funded by the population.
"Now the proposed model of this criticism should be avoided, because it is proposed to finance the protection of animals with increased income in the environmental protection support programme and the right of labelling not only to veterinary surgeons, but also to publicists, i.e. persons of animal care institutions who have completed special courses at the Veterinary Academy," the authors of the project say.
The draft law has been registered by a group of members of the Seimas representing different parliamentary groups - Kęstutis Mažeika, Algimantas Dumbrava, Eugenijus Gentvilas, Edmundas Pupinis, Rimas Andrikis, Antanas Vinkus and others.
