Restrictions on co-generating power plants complain to the Constitutional Court
The opposition of the Seimas, as promised, to the Constitutional Court has appealed to the Constitutional Court the decision of the ruling parties to adopt a law on the possibility of stopping the construction of waste incineration plants.
On Tuesday, an opposition faction of Homeland Union-Lithuanian Christian democrats (TS-LKD) announced that a group of members of the Sejm had appealed to the Constitutional Court, requesting an investigation into the compliance of the amendments to the Law on Waste Management adopted by the ruling parties with the Constitution.
"Parlamentarians emphasize that the provisions of the law are aimed at questioning the projects of waste incinerators that have already been implemented, entrusting the Government with new restrictions (20 km distance from the residential area) and the entry into force of the law on waste incineration plants in Vilnius and Kaunas," it said in a statement.
"Parliamenrians point out that in view of how the law formulates a ban on the construction of objects of national importance for waste management (i.e. the distance is determined and the concept of "living territory" is defined), it must be concluded that this prohibition is disproportionately limited or even denigitably the essence of the right to carry out economic activities related to the incineration of waste for the production of energy, because the established criteria were formulated in such a way that, taking into account the denseness and distribution of the Lithuanian population in the country.
According to the application, the adopted law is in conflict with the constitutional principle of the rule of law.
"The CT has repeatedly stated that the legislation does not apply to the legal facts that have taken place which arose prior to the entry into force of the newly adopted legal act.
In this case, the Government is empowered to apply the law retroactively, according to an indestructible criterion.
Having established such a legal regulation violated the constitutional principles of legal certainty, legal certainty, protection of legal expectations, and therefore the constitutional principle of the rule of law is violated," the appeal states.
It also notes that significant financial investments have already been made in the projects of co-generating power plants of Vilnius and Kaunas.
Suspension of the projects currently being carried out would incur not only hundreds of hundreds of millions of financial losses in the state, the residents of Lithuania would also incur huge losses - heating prices would increase, environmental pollution would lead to an increase.
Receipt to KKT was signed by members of the Seimas TS-LKD, Liberal Movement, Social Democrat groups and vice-president Algirdas Butevicius, deputy elder of the Social Democrat Labor faction.
A couple of weeks ago, the Seimas was able to collect 71 votes by the ruling votes in order to dismantle the amendments to the Law on Waste Management adopted by President Dalia Grybauskaite, anticipating the government’s right to halt the construction of cogenerative power plants that have already been started, as well as established a 20-kilometre-to-renovation zone for the “quarantine” zone for the construction of such power plants.
Amendments to the law re-adopted by the Seimas will come into force on 1 January 2019. However, the ruling parties are already proposing to waive the restriction of 20 km.
It is planned to consider this proposal in the autumn session of the Seimas.
However, there are no plans to waive the government's right to suspend the already launched projects.
KT will deal with this case in order - not earlier than in a year's time.
