The President's Office: There's No Need for Artificial Facilitation on a Referendum
After the Seimas approves the proposal of the ruling parties to reduce the referendum cartel to the first section of the Constitution that currently has a greater protection, the president will veto the amendments with the amendments, its adviser Mindaugas Lingė said.
"More than half of the population voted in favor of the constitution - that's the requirement.
This and its change, especially the articles contained in its first section, should certainly not have a smaller majority.
It's not hard to predict that the president will definitely veto," Dalia Grybauskaitė's domestic policy adviser told Žinių Radijas radio on Tuesday.
He emphasized that in the first section of the Constitution defines the fundamental things of the state - the language, the flag, the territory, the capital, the sovereignty of the state, thus reducing the number of votes necessary to replace these articles, according to M. Lingė, a "negligible precedent" would be created and "to open uncontrollable risks".
"In the opinion of the President, there is no need for any artificial facilitations, it is more necessary to prepare for the referendum itself, there is a need for more dialogue with people," he insisted.
The Seimas is scheduled to vote Tuesday on the adoption of an amendment to the Law on Referendum.
Therein provides that a decision on the amendments to the First and Fourteenth Separation of the Constitution with higher protection shall be taken if at least two-fifths of the citizens who have the right-of-voting in the framework of the referendum are approved.
At the same time, more than half of all citizens who have the right to vote can be replaced by a referendum to the articles of the first and fourteenth section of the Constitution.
Approximately 2.5 million citizens have the right to vote in Lithuania: half would be 1.25 million electorate, two-fifth - a million voters.
This proposal was born after the Seimas in September approved the president’s veto on amendments previously adopted in the parliament, relaxing the requirements concerning the number of votes when amending Article 12 of the First Section of the Constitution defining citizenship.
It is planned to reduce the threshold for the referendum in the run-up to dual-citizenship, it is expected to take place on May 12 and May 26 next year, when the first and second rounds of the presidential election are planned.
Emigrants who emigrated after the restoration of their independence on March 11, 1990, at the same time, with some of the exceptions, may not have dual citizenship.
