Lithuanian Social Democrat Labor (LSDDDP), headed vice-chairwoman Gediminas Kirkilas, which was founded in spring this spring, is not yet earmarked to allocate funds from the budget.
However, they can be allocated already in the near future by a separate decision of the Central Electoral Commission, subject to a change in the legal environment regulating the distribution of grants to parties.
Laura Matjošaitytė, the chairwoman of the Central Electoral Commission, informed VŽ that "this meeting of the commission is scheduled to take a decision on the half-military allocation of the budget grant to the parties".
According to her, the Central Electoral Commission has to make a decision on this issue by November 15.
It envisages the distribution of EUR 2.751 million to seven parties, six of which are parliamentary.
The Oppositive Homeland Union-Lithuanian Christian Democrats should receive more than EUR 0.66 million, the ruling Peasant-Green Union - about 0.55-million euros, the opposition Social-Democratic Party - about 0.5 million euros, the Opposition Liberal Movement - almost 0.4 million euros, in the Sejm - about EUR 0.23 million for the opposition Liberal Party, which has not had its faction - about EUR 0.23 million, recently joined to the ruling party.
According to the current law, which the Central Electoral Commission will follow when allocating budget money, grants are awarded to parties that meet the requirements of the law and have received at least 3% of the votes in the last elections of the Seimas, municipal councils and the European Parliament.
Therefore, the current legal regulation does not allow the LSDDP, which was established in the spring this year to expect to receive money from the budget.
This party has not yet participated in a single election.
