President Dalia Grybauskaite vetoed the respective amendments adopted by political parties and this year's budget laws.
The Seimas has not yet made up its decision on this veto, but the representatives of the ruling P. Kirkilas and Ramūnas Karbauskis, the leader of the peasant-greens, say that the parliamentarians will seek to dismiss the president’s contradictions.
However, it will not be possible to do this until this Thursday’s CEC meeting, therefore, according to Matjošaitytė, “attachments will be divided according to the legal order that has existed so far”.
The head of the Central Electoral Commission did not rule that if the Seimas dissuaded the president’s veto this or next week and a new amendment on the allocation of budgetary funds to the parties came into force, a decision may be taken into force in the commission about the appointment of an additional grant to individual political forces, including the LSDDP.
"When making a decision, we will take into account the entire current legal environment, because we are an executive body with the implementation of the adopted laws," she said.
If the Sejm was able to dismiss a veto and the new laws come into force, according to them, the Government would be separated by the government, which additionally borrowed the corresponding amounts.
Subsidies already allocated to the seven parties would not suffer as a result of such a decision.
