You already want to teach in schools differently: promises fewer tests, more practice
Such changes in the content of education are planned by the Ministry of Education and Science, which on Monday presented them at the forum "Changes of educational content: foreign experience for decisions in Lithuania".
Jurgita Petrauskienė, Minister of Education and Science, who spoke during the discussion, stated that more than 30 million euros have been invested in the refurbishment of the educational content over the past ten years.
"According to the results of the International Survey of Pupil Achievements in PISA, the achievements of schoolchildren are below the average of OECD countries," said Jurgita Petrauskienė, Minister of Education and Science.
OECD expert Phil Lambert, cited in a statement from the Ministry of Education and Science, stated that Lithuania is too oriented towards the provision of knowledge rather than the practical application of knowledge.
According to him, general curiosity programs in Lithuania pay a lot of attention to the literacy, critical thinking, respect and guerrilla, but it is also necessary to develop children's creativity, entrepreneurship, financial literacy and empathy.
Gražvydas Kazakevičius, the Vice-Minister of Education and Science, while presenting the changes in the content of general education, stated that the scope of the programmes starting to be renovated during this academic year will be declining, they will be more specific, the disciplines are more strongly interconnected with each other.
According to the Vice-Minister, pupil education will be more individualized, the schools will become more self-contained - they will be allowed to prepare from 10 to 30 per cent of the content of their unique education, the current pupil evaluation system will be changed to a cumulative punctuation, more weight will be gained by the student's competences acquired in a non-formal way.
Decrease in standardized tests.
It is expected that the general curricula will be digital, user-friendly, linked to e-resources, tasksbank.
Renovulsing educational content aims to improve students' achievements in the international PISA survey by at at least 10 per cent, by one-threat the gap between students' achievements would be reduced by a third, students would acquire modern competences in the school, be litered in various fields.
Lithuania plans to allocate 7.8 million euros of funds from the European Union and the state-budget to update the educational content from May 2018.
It is anticipated that in the 2019-2020 school year, the project of renovated general-educational curricula will be tested in some schools, and from September 2021 they will be implemented in all schools.
