In Lithuania - an exercise on how to respond to an extreme tidal
A ten-day-to-day exercise is launched in Lithuania on Monday to improve institutions' responsidiary quilt.
According to the Fire and Resistance and Rescue Department (PAGD), it will be screened during the exercises how state institutions are prepared to work in the country after declaring a state-level state-level emergency, such as flooding: to eliminate its consequences, to coordinate the search for people, rescue and urgent eviction from submersible areas, to organize their supply of drinking water, food, to ensure the necessary supply of energy and to take care of other means of protection of the population.
More than 100 representatives from all ministries of the country, other institutions, as well as members of 12-municipal emergency operation centres will take part in the exercise.
On November 5-13 there will be a preparatory stage of the exercise, during which the municipality, having received information from the Hydrometeorological Service of the PRT, will take decisions on the management of an emergency.
The active phase of the exercise is scheduled for November 14-15.
During it, the State Emergency Operations Center will operate in the department, and in the state and municipal institutions there will be emergency operations centers of institutions.
After the government declared a state-level state-level emergency due to the consequences of flooding, the head of operations appointed by the prime minister will lead the elimination of an emergency, the elimination of its consequences, actions for the protection and rescue of residents and assets.
Mindaugas Kanapickas, deputy director of the PAGD, says that the most important thing in the exercise is to check the readiness of the institutions to perform the functions provided for in legal acts, to assess the possibilities to exchange the information, to check how to warn and inform residents, state and municipal institutions, institutions and enterprises and to "improve the emergency management skills of state institutions".
The exercise will also demonstrate the joint capacity of Lithuanian, Latvian-Estonian-Estonian-rescuers to pump a huge amount of water from flooded areas.
For this purpose, BaltFloodCombat module was established in 2009.
