Governors no longer require a distance of 20 km when circulating waste incinerators
Vilnius cogenerative power plant is under construction, near Gariūnai market.
Residents no longer strive to maintain a 20-kilometre distance from the settlements when structuring waste incinerators.
Kęstutis Mažeika, the headmaster of the Committee on Environmental Protection, registered an amendment to the Law on Waste Management in the Seimas on Monday renouncing such a requirement.
According to P. Mažeika, the "Farmers" faction, having previously maintained a provision on a distance of 20 km, promises to maintain an amendment regarding its refusal.
Ms. Mažeika's amendment stipulates that as of January 2019, waste incineration power plants could be built under environmental protection and public health requirements.
He acknowledges that the amendments were prompted by the president's doubts about such a ban.
"Given the definition of the residential area, it is obvious that it would be almost impossible to find such a place where the above-mentioned objects could be built," the chairman of the committee said.
The ruling majority in October resisted the presidential veto's amendments that tighten the construction of waste incinerators.
In addition to the ban on the construction of waste incinerators closer than 20 km from the settlements, the amendments envisage allowed the Government to take decisions on the future of two power plants already under construction, taking into account public health interests, taking into account public health interests.
Recent provisions Mr. Mažeika does not propose adjustments.
The energy group Lietuvos Energija builds a waste-and-biofuel-fire-fired power plant in Vilnius, together with Fortum Lithuania, a Finnish energy concern Fortum Lithuania.
The latter already incinerates the amount of waste in Klaipėda.
Possibly a Comprehensive Compr-Morning in Brexit Negotiations
Britain's Irish leaders on Monday discussed a state-of-contemporary communeration on a challenging issue in Northern Ireland stalling Brexit talks, Dublin said.
Speaking by phone, British Prime Minister Theresa May has offered the possibility to review the so-called Hedging Facility Arrangement mechanism, which would allow an open-minded border between Britain's Northern Irish province and the Republic of Ireland, a member of the European Union.
Irish Prime Minister Leo Varadkar has vowed to consider the proposal, but has ruled out any possibility for Britain to unilaterally withdraw from the agreement, the Irish government said in a statement.
In her conversation, Mrs May "explained the possibility of reviewing the hedging measures mechanism," she said.
"Varadkar made it possible to understand that there is an open review of the new proposals, on the condition that the outcome of such a review does not relate to a unilateral decision to terminate the hedging measures," the government said in a statement.
Brexit negotiators do not find an admissible solution on how to evade checks at the UK-Ireland border when Britain leaves the single market and customs union in March next year.
The two parties agree on "cushion-related measures" to maintain the border between the Republic of Ireland and Northern Ireland until a larger-scale trade agreement has been signed to ensure that additional customs controls at the border are not enforced.
The EU wants Northern Ireland to continue to abide by EU customs rules, but London wants it to be applied provisionally to the United Kingdom as a whole.
"The Daily Telegraph" reported on Monday that Brexit Secretary Dominic Raab is pushing for Britain to be able to withdraw from the Hedging-Organisation Agreement unilaterally after three months.
Varadkar, however, told reporters in Dublin that his government "couldn't support it."
"Three-minded remedies for three-month-a-month or limited term are not worth the paper on which it will be listed," he said.
In the past, London and Brussels have not been able to confirm information about a breakthrough in the Brexit talks following a report in Britain over the weekend that the deal is within reach.
"I'd say we're continuing to make progress in the negotiations, but there's still work to be done," Mrs May's spokesman in London said.
Margaritis Schinas, a spokesman for the European Commission, also told reporters: "We're not there yet."
Nobel Laureates Call for Reliable Reliable Information
A group of prominent thinkers, including Nobel laureates, issued a declaration on Monday, calling for the right of access to reliable information in the era of the internet.
Among those who signed the International Declaration on Information and Democracies Include Economists Amartya Old and Joseph Stiglitz, Nobel Peace Prize-winning Iranian lawyer and human rights activist Shirin Ebadi, as well as Nobel Prize-winning writer Mario Vorgas Llosa).
"We urge goodwill leaders to take action on all continents in order to bolster democraciecratic models and open public debate, through which citizens can make decisions based on facts," the group said in a statement.
They arguing that "global space of information and communication" is a common good that "must be guarded in order to bolster the freedom of self-expression and views."
"People have a fundamental right to receive information that is freely collected, processed and circulated, building on the basis of a commitment to the truth, the pluralism principles of viewpoints and the rational methods of fact-finding," the authors of the declaration added.
The document asks for help from world leaders, including U.S. President Donald Trump, who often criticises "fat news" but regularly accuses the distortion of facts.
World leaders are expected to express their stance on the issue, when they will gather for the "Peace Forum in Paris" on November 11-13, the signatories called.
The declaration was drafted within two months under Ebadi and Christophe Deloire (Christof Deluar), the head of the Paris-based media freedom-ridden human rights organization Reporters Without Borders (RSF).
Other signatories include former Senegal's former President Abdous Dyof, Chinese lawyer and human rights Activist Teng Biao and political scientist Francis Fukuyama (Frensis Fukuyama).
Sejm Approves Severe Penalties for Harmful Produces
The Seimas has approved amendments to the Law on the Safety of Goods anticipating that fines should be increased.
The amendments were unanimously approved in the meeting room.
Antanas Vinkus, a member of the Seimas Committee on Health Affairs, said, when it comes to the new project, it should be welcomed that the objectives set are related to the improved protection of consumers of products.
In addition, its provisions shall be adapted to the requirements of the decision of the European Parliament and of the Council.
"In my opinion, the proposed draft law distributes more and more concretely to all exaggerators involved in the supply and distribution process: manufacturers, service providers, importers, distributors and others.
As practice shows, the current system of fines does not always prevent further infringements, because the entities, having paid the fines, continue to commit re-alert violations," A. Vinkus said.
The project was drafted by the Ministry of Economy, after the approval of the Government, the Seimas Economics Committee supplemented it with its own proposals.
Amendments to the law are aimed at increasing fines for violations, strengthening the protection of consumer rights, granting more power to market regulators, and to create a clear system for the maintenance of product safety requirements and their compliance.
According according to the Ministry of Economy, there is currently no obligation for manufacturers to properly label the product and thus ensure its traceability.
In addition, according to the ministry, market surveillance authorities did not have the right not to fall within the territory, buildings, premises of manufacturers and suppliers, to carry out inspections, which complicates the work of the authorities.
According to the Ministry’s assessment, the six-month time-limit for the imposition of fines and warnings is too short to establish an infringement, carry out an investigation and an imposition of a fine.
Also, according to the ministries, the imposed fines are not sufficiently effective.
Companies often repeatedly violate the requirements of the law - 14 companies did so in 2017 alone.
The Ministry of Economy has proposed to establish an obligation for manufacturers to properly label the product in order to ensure its traceability in order to make it easier for market regulators to identify suppliers of non-compliant products.
It is also proposed to grant market surveillance authorities the right to carry out checks on inspections carried out in the course of state product safety control by economic operators that manufacture, place products on the market, buildings, premises.
Penalties are expected to be increased for violating the law.
Producer, importer or authorised representative who has placed on the market of dangerous products and a distributor who has placed on the market shall be fined between EUR 150 up to EUR 1,500 (now 144 and 1 448).
If the State Consumer Rights Protection Authority (VVTAT) or other market surveillance authority decides to prohibit the placing and placing on the market of dangerous products or the provision of dangerous services would impose a fine of between EUR 1 000 and EUR 5 000 (now 868 to 4 344).
Failure to comply with the requirement to remove dangerous products from the market would be fined between EUR 2500 and EUR 6 500 (now between EUR 1 4448 and EUR 5 79).
If dangerous products have caused damage to the consumer's health, a fine of between EUR 2,500 and EUR 15 000 (now between EUR 1 448 and 11584 would be fined.
If dangerous products caused the death of the consumer, a fine of EUR 6,500 to 25,000 (now 5 792 to 23 169) would be fined.
According to the data of the Ministry, a fine of up to 25 thousand euros (minimum the amount of the fine has not been imposed in Poland and Austria for violating legal acts regulating product safety (minimum amount has not been established), in Belgium - a fine of 26 euros up to 600 thousand euros, in Romania - a fine of 155 euros of 6 666 euros, in Bulgaria - a fine of 125 euros to 7,500 euros.
